# ---------------- JavaScript / TypeScript ----------------
q("javascript", "What's the difference between let, const and var in JavaScript?", "`var` is function-scoped and hoisted (initialised as undefined), which causes surprising bugs. `let` and `const` are block-scoped and can't be used before their declaration. `const` prevents reassigning the variable, but the object it points to can still be mutated. Default to `const`, use `let` when you must reassign, and avoid `var`.")
q("javascript", "What is a closure?", "A closure is a function that remembers the variables from the scope where it was created, even after that scope has finished. For example:\n\n```js\nfunction counter() {\n  let n = 0;\n  return () => ++n;\n}\nconst next = counter();\nnext(); // 1\nnext(); // 2\n```\n\nThe returned function keeps access to `n`. Closures power callbacks, private state and many patterns in React hooks.")
q("javascript", "== vs === in JavaScript?", "`===` compares value and type with no conversion, so `1 === '1'` is false. `==` converts types first using complicated rules, so `0 == ''` and `null == undefined` are true, which is why it's easy to get surprised. Use `===` by default; the one common exception is `x == null`, which checks for both null and undefined.")
q("javascript", "How do Promises and async/await relate?", "A Promise represents a value that will arrive later. `async/await` is syntax on top of promises: an `async` function returns a promise, and `await` pauses that function until a promise settles, letting asynchronous code read top to bottom. Errors become exceptions you can catch with `try/catch`.\n\n```js\nasync function load() {\n  try {\n    const res = await fetch('/api/items');\n    return await res.json();\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error('failed', err);\n  }\n}\n```")
q("javascript", "Why does `await` inside a forEach not wait?", "`forEach` ignores the promises returned by its callback, so it doesn't wait for them. Use a `for...of` loop when you need sequential awaiting, or `await Promise.all(items.map(async item => ...))` to run them in parallel and wait for all.")
q("javascript", "What is the event loop?", "JavaScript runs one piece of code at a time on a single thread. When you start async work (timers, network requests), the browser or Node handles it in the background and queues a callback. The event loop takes callbacks from the queue and runs them when the call stack is empty. Promise callbacks (microtasks) run before timer callbacks (macrotasks), which explains the order of `setTimeout(fn, 0)` versus `Promise.resolve().then(fn)`.")
q("javascript", "What does `this` refer to in JavaScript?", "It depends on how a function is called, not where it's defined. Called as `obj.method()`, `this` is `obj`. Called plainly, it's `undefined` in strict mode (or the global object otherwise). Arrow functions don't have their own `this`; they use the one from the surrounding scope, which is why they're handy for callbacks inside methods.")
q("javascript", "Explain map, filter and reduce.", "They're array methods that transform data without mutating the original. `map` produces a new array by applying a function to each item, `filter` keeps items for which a function returns true, and `reduce` folds the array into a single value.\n\n```js\nconst nums = [1, 2, 3, 4];\nnums.map(n => n * 2);          // [2, 4, 6, 8]\nnums.filter(n => n % 2 === 0); // [2, 4]\nnums.reduce((a, n) => a + n, 0); // 10\n```")
q("javascript", "What is destructuring?", "Destructuring unpacks values from arrays or objects into variables: `const { name, age = 18 } = user;` and `const [first, ...rest] = list;`. You can rename (`{ name: userName }`), set defaults, and use it in function parameters, which makes code shorter and clearer.")
q("javascript", "What's the spread operator used for?", "`...` expands an iterable or object in place. Use it to copy or combine arrays (`[...a, ...b]`), to shallow-copy and override objects (`{ ...user, name: 'Sam' }`), and to pass array items as arguments (`Math.max(...nums)`). It's a shallow copy, so nested objects are still shared.")
q("javascript", "What does optional chaining do?", "`user?.address?.city` returns `undefined` instead of throwing if `user` or `address` is null or undefined. Combine it with nullish coalescing to provide defaults: `user?.address?.city ?? 'Unknown'`. Unlike `||`, `??` only falls back for null and undefined, not for `0` or an empty string.")
q("javascript", "How do I deep clone an object in JavaScript?", "Use `structuredClone(obj)`, available in modern browsers and Node. It handles nested objects, arrays, dates, maps and sets. The old `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj))` trick drops functions, `undefined` and dates and fails on cycles. A spread copy (`{...obj}`) is only shallow.")
q("javascript", "What is hoisting?", "Declarations are processed before the code runs, so function declarations can be called before they appear, and `var` variables exist (as undefined) before their line. `let` and `const` are hoisted too but sit in a 'temporal dead zone' where access throws a ReferenceError until the declaration is reached.")
q("javascript", "What's the difference between null and undefined?", "`undefined` means a variable exists but has no value yet (also what missing properties and omitted arguments give you). `null` is an explicit 'no value' that a programmer assigns. `typeof null` is `'object'`, a long-standing historical quirk.")
q("javascript", "How do I debounce a function?", "Debouncing delays running a function until a quiet period has passed, which is useful for search-as-you-type.\n\n```js\nfunction debounce(fn, ms) {\n  let timer;\n  return (...args) => {\n    clearTimeout(timer);\n    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);\n  };\n}\nconst onInput = debounce(search, 300);\n```\n\nThrottling is the sibling idea: run at most once per interval instead of waiting for silence.")
q("javascript", "What are ES modules?", "ES modules are the standard module system: `export` things from a file and `import` them elsewhere. Imports are static, so tools can tree-shake unused code. Use `import x from './x.js'` in browsers and modern Node (with `\"type\": \"module\"` or `.mjs`). The older CommonJS style uses `require()` and `module.exports`.")
q("javascript", "What is the difference between localStorage, sessionStorage and cookies?", "`localStorage` persists until cleared, `sessionStorage` lasts for the tab session, and both are synchronous key-value stores of strings limited to a few MB and readable by any script on the origin. Cookies are sent with every matching HTTP request and can be marked `HttpOnly` (hidden from scripts), `Secure` and `SameSite`. Don't store auth tokens in localStorage if XSS is a concern; HttpOnly cookies are safer.")
q("javascript", "How does fetch handle errors?", "`fetch` only rejects on network failures. An HTTP 404 or 500 still resolves, so you must check `response.ok` yourself:\n\n```js\nconst res = await fetch(url);\nif (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);\nconst data = await res.json();\n```")
q("javascript", "Why does 0.1 + 0.2 !== 0.3 in JavaScript?", "Numbers are IEEE-754 double-precision binary floats, and 0.1 and 0.2 can't be represented exactly, so their sum is 0.30000000000000004. Compare with a tolerance (`Math.abs(a - b) < Number.EPSILON`) or work in integers (cents) for money.")
q("typescript", "What's the point of TypeScript?", "TypeScript adds static types to JavaScript. The compiler catches mistakes like calling a function with the wrong argument or misspelling a property before the code runs, improves editor autocomplete and documents intent. The types are erased when compiled, so there's no runtime cost, but they also don't validate data coming from outside (like API responses), which still needs runtime checks.")
q("typescript", "interface vs type in TypeScript?", "They overlap heavily. `interface` is best for object shapes and can be extended and merged across declarations; `type` can also name unions, tuples and mapped types (`type Id = string | number`). A practical rule: use `interface` for public object contracts and `type` for everything else, and be consistent within a codebase.")
q("typescript", "What does `unknown` do compared with `any`?", "`any` turns off type checking for a value. `unknown` means 'could be anything', but you must narrow it (with `typeof`, `instanceof` or a validator) before using it. Prefer `unknown` for data you don't control, such as parsed JSON.")
q("typescript", "How do generics work in TypeScript?", "Generics let a function or type work over many types while keeping the relationship between them:\n\n```ts\nfunction first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {\n  return items[0];\n}\nconst n = first([1, 2, 3]);      // number | undefined\nconst s = first(['a', 'b']);     // string | undefined\n```\n\nThe `T` is filled in by inference, so the return type matches the input.")
q("typescript", "What are union types and type narrowing?", "A union (`string | number`) says a value may be one of several types. Narrowing refines it inside a branch:\n\n```ts\nfunction len(x: string | string[]) {\n  return typeof x === 'string' ? x.length : x.length; // both work here, but each branch knows its type\n}\n```\n\nDiscriminated unions (a shared literal `kind` field) make `switch` statements exhaustive and type-safe.")
q("typescript", "How do I type an async function that returns JSON from an API?", "Declare the return type as `Promise<YourType>`, and remember the type is a claim, not a check:\n\n```ts\ninterface User { id: number; name: string }\n\nasync function getUser(id: number): Promise<User> {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);\n  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('failed');\n  return (await res.json()) as User;\n}\n```\n\nFor safety, validate the shape at runtime with a schema library such as zod.")
q("typescript", "What does `strict` mode enable?", "The `strict` flag turns on a family of checks, including `strictNullChecks` (null and undefined are separate types), `noImplicitAny`, and `strictFunctionTypes`. It catches many real bugs, such as accessing a property on a possibly-undefined value, and is recommended for new projects.")

# ---------------- React / frontend ----------------
q("react", "What is the virtual DOM?", "React keeps a lightweight in-memory description of the UI. When state changes, it builds a new description, compares it with the previous one (diffing) and applies only the minimal real DOM changes. The benefit isn't that it's always faster than hand-written DOM code, but that you describe what the UI should look like and React works out how to update it.")
q("react", "When does a React component re-render?", "When its state changes, when its parent re-renders (unless memoised), or when a context it consumes changes. A re-render means calling the component function again, not necessarily touching the DOM. Avoid optimising until you see a real problem; then use `React.memo`, `useMemo` or restructuring state to be local.")
q("react", "How does useEffect work and what is the dependency array?", "`useEffect` runs side effects after render (fetching, subscriptions, timers). The dependency array lists values the effect uses: it re-runs when any change; `[]` runs once after mount; omitting it runs after every render. Return a cleanup function to unsubscribe or cancel.\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const id = setInterval(tick, 1000);\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\n}, []);\n```")
q("react", "Why is my useEffect running twice in development?", "In React's Strict Mode (development only) components mount, unmount and remount once to expose effects that aren't cleaned up properly. It's not a bug in production. If running twice breaks something, the effect probably needs a cleanup function or shouldn't be an effect at all.")
q("react", "State vs props?", "Props are inputs passed from a parent and are read-only inside the child. State is data a component owns and can change over time with `setState`. When state changes, React re-renders that component and passes new props to its children. Data flows down; events and callbacks flow up.")
q("react", "Why shouldn't I mutate state directly in React?", "React detects changes by comparing references. If you mutate an array or object in place and set it back, the reference is the same, so React may not re-render and memoised children won't update. Create new values instead: `setItems([...items, newItem])` or `setUser({ ...user, name })`.")
q("react", "What is lifting state up?", "When two components need the same data, move the state to their closest common parent and pass it down as props, along with callbacks to change it. It keeps a single source of truth, which avoids copies of data going out of sync.")
q("react", "What is the key prop for?", "Keys let React identify which list items changed, were added or removed. Use a stable unique id from your data. Using the array index as a key can cause bugs (wrong input state, animations) when items are reordered or inserted.")
q("react", "useMemo vs useCallback?", "`useMemo` caches a computed value between renders; `useCallback` caches a function identity. Both only help when the cached result is expensive to recompute or is passed to a memoised child where a changed reference would cause extra work. Using them everywhere adds complexity without benefit.")
q("react", "When should I use useReducer instead of useState?", "Use `useReducer` when state has several related fields or the next state depends on complex rules. A reducer centralises updates as actions, which makes transitions explicit and easy to test: `dispatch({ type: 'add', item })`.")
q("react", "What is context and when is it a bad idea?", "Context passes data through the component tree without threading props at each level, good for things like theme, current user or locale. It re-renders all consumers when its value changes, so don't put rapidly changing state in a single big context; split contexts or use a state library.")
q("react", "How do I fetch data in React properly?", "For anything beyond a demo, use a data-fetching library such as TanStack Query. It handles loading and error states, caching, deduplication, retries and refetching, all of which are error-prone when done with raw `useEffect`. If you do use `useEffect`, handle cleanup (ignore stale responses) and errors.")
q("react", "Controlled vs uncontrolled inputs?", "A controlled input's value lives in React state (`value` and `onChange`), so React is the source of truth and you can validate on every keystroke. An uncontrolled input keeps its own value in the DOM and you read it via a ref or on submit. Controlled is the default for forms that need validation; uncontrolled is fine for simple forms.")
q("react", "What are custom hooks?", "A custom hook is a function starting with `use` that calls other hooks to package reusable stateful logic, e.g. `useDebounce(value, ms)` or `useLocalStorage(key)`. Each component that calls it gets its own independent state.")
q("react", "What is prop drilling and how do I avoid it?", "Prop drilling is passing a prop through many layers that don't use it. Options: composition (pass the rendered child as `children`), context for truly global data, or a state library for shared client state. Don't reach for context until the drilling is actually painful.")
q("frontend", "What is the difference between a CSS class selector and an ID selector, and which should I prefer?", "An ID (`#header`) targets a single unique element and has higher specificity; a class (`.card`) can be reused on many elements. Prefer classes for styling because they're reusable and keep specificity low and predictable; reserve IDs for anchors and form associations.")
q("frontend", "Flexbox or Grid?", "Flexbox is one-dimensional: arrange items in a row or a column and distribute space between them. Grid is two-dimensional: define rows and columns and place items in the grid. A good rule is to use Grid for page-level layouts and Flexbox for aligning content within components, and they combine well.")
q("frontend", "How do I make a layout responsive?", "Start mobile-first with fluid widths (percentages, `fr` units, `max-width`), use media queries (or container queries) to adjust at breakpoints where the content breaks rather than at specific device sizes, make images flexible (`max-width: 100%`), and set `<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">`. Test at narrow widths.")
q("frontend", "What is the difference between margin and padding?", "Padding is the space inside an element, between its content and its border (the background shows through it). Margin is the space outside the border, separating the element from its neighbours. Vertical margins between siblings can collapse into one.")
q("frontend", "How can I improve a slow web page?", "Measure first (Lighthouse, the browser performance panel). Common wins: ship less JavaScript (code-splitting, removing unused dependencies), compress and properly size images and use modern formats, lazy-load below-the-fold content, cache static assets with long lifetimes, reduce layout shifts by reserving image sizes, and avoid blocking the main thread with heavy synchronous work.")
q("frontend", "What is accessibility (a11y) basics for developers?", "Use semantic HTML (`button`, `nav`, `label`) so assistive technology understands the page, make everything usable from the keyboard with visible focus, give images meaningful `alt` text, ensure sufficient colour contrast, label form fields, and don't rely on colour alone. Test with a screen reader and keyboard only.")

# ---------------- web backend / HTTP / APIs ----------------
q("backend", "What is REST?", "REST is an architectural style for web APIs: resources are identified by URLs (`/users/42`), and standard HTTP methods act on them (GET to read, POST to create, PUT/PATCH to update, DELETE to remove). Servers are stateless between requests, and responses use status codes and a consistent format (usually JSON). 'RESTful' in practice mostly means following these conventions.")
q("backend", "PUT vs PATCH vs POST?", "POST creates a resource (or triggers an action) and is not idempotent. PUT replaces a resource entirely and is idempotent. PATCH applies a partial update. Idempotent means repeating the same request leaves the same final state, which matters for retries.")
q("backend", "Which HTTP status codes should I know?", "200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content; 301/302 redirects; 400 Bad Request (malformed), 401 Unauthorized (not authenticated), 403 Forbidden (authenticated but not allowed), 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict, 422 Unprocessable Entity (validation failed), 429 Too Many Requests; 500 Internal Server Error, 502/503/504 for gateway and availability problems. Pick the most specific code and keep error bodies consistent.")
q("backend", "What is CORS and why do I get CORS errors?", "Browsers block a page from reading responses from a different origin (scheme, host or port) unless the server opts in with CORS headers such as `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. The error shows in the browser console, but it's the server that must be configured; the request often reaches the server anyway. In development you can use a proxy so the browser sees one origin.")
q("backend", "Authentication vs authorization?", "Authentication answers 'who are you?' (logging in, verifying a token). Authorization answers 'what are you allowed to do?' (permissions, roles, ownership checks). Do both on the server for every request, never trust the client to enforce either.")
q("backend", "How do JWTs work, and what are the pitfalls?", "A JSON Web Token is a signed string containing claims (user id, expiry). The server verifies the signature instead of looking up a session. Pitfalls: they can't be revoked easily before expiry, so keep access tokens short-lived and use refresh tokens; the payload is encoded, not encrypted, so don't put secrets in it; always verify the signature and expiry and fix the allowed algorithm.")
q("backend", "How should passwords be stored?", "Never store them in plain text or with a fast hash. Use a slow, salted password hashing algorithm such as bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2, which makes brute force expensive; the library generates the salt for you. Add rate limiting and consider multi-factor authentication.")
q("backend", "What is middleware?", "Middleware is code that sits in the request pipeline and runs before and/or after your route handler: logging, authentication, CORS, rate limiting, error handling. Each piece can modify the request or response or stop the chain. In FastAPI and Express it's added with `app.add_middleware` or `app.use`.")
q("backend", "What is a database migration?", "A migration is a versioned script that changes the database schema (add a column, create a table) in a repeatable way, so every environment can be brought to the same structure. Tools like Alembic or Flyway track which migrations have been applied. Never edit applied migrations; add a new one.")
q("backend", "What's the N+1 query problem?", "It happens when code runs one query to get N items and then one more query for each item (for example, loading each order's customer separately), producing N+1 round trips. Fix it with a join, eager loading (`selectinload` in SQLAlchemy, `include` in Prisma) or a batch query for all related ids.")
q("backend", "How do I add pagination to an API?", "Accept `limit` and `offset` (or a `cursor`) parameters, cap the maximum limit, return the items plus enough metadata for the next page (a total, or a `next_cursor`). Cursor-based pagination stays fast and consistent on large, changing datasets; offset is simpler for small ones.")
q("backend", "What is rate limiting?", "It restricts how many requests a client can make in a time window to protect the service from abuse or accidents, returning `429 Too Many Requests` with a `Retry-After` hint. Common algorithms are fixed window, sliding window and token bucket. Apply stricter limits to login and other sensitive routes.")
q("backend", "What does idempotency mean for APIs?", "An operation is idempotent if doing it several times has the same effect as doing it once. GET, PUT and DELETE should be. POST usually isn't, so for things like payments clients send an idempotency key and the server remembers it, which makes retries after timeouts safe.")
q("backend", "What are webhooks?", "A webhook is an HTTP callback: instead of you polling a service, it sends a POST to your URL when an event happens. Verify the signature, respond quickly with a 2xx, process the work asynchronously, and be prepared for duplicate or out-of-order deliveries.")
q("backend", "WebSockets vs Server-Sent Events vs polling?", "Polling repeatedly asks the server for updates and is simplest but wasteful. Server-Sent Events keep a one-way connection from server to browser over HTTP, which suits live feeds and streaming text. WebSockets give a two-way persistent connection, best for chat, games and collaborative editing. Choose the simplest that fits.")
q("fastapi", "How do I define a route with validation in FastAPI?", "Use Pydantic models for bodies and type hints for parameters; FastAPI validates automatically and generates docs.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nclass TaskIn(BaseModel):\n    title: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=120)\n    done: bool = False\n\n@app.post(\"/tasks\", status_code=201)\ndef create_task(task: TaskIn):\n    return {\"id\": 1, **task.model_dump()}\n```\n\nInvalid input returns a 422 response with details.")
q("fastapi", "What is dependency injection in FastAPI?", "`Depends` lets you declare reusable pieces a route needs, like a database session or the current user, and FastAPI calls them for each request.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import Depends\n\ndef get_db():\n    db = SessionLocal()\n    try:\n        yield db\n    finally:\n        db.close()\n\n@app.get(\"/items\")\ndef items(db = Depends(get_db)):\n    ...\n```\n\nIt also makes testing easy, because you can override a dependency with a fake.")
q("fastapi", "async def or def for FastAPI routes?", "Use `async def` when the handler awaits non-blocking I/O (async database drivers, httpx). Use plain `def` for blocking code; FastAPI runs it in a thread pool so it won't freeze the event loop. Putting blocking calls (like `requests` or a sync DB driver) inside `async def` is the classic mistake, because it blocks every other request.")
q("fastapi", "How do I handle errors in FastAPI?", "Raise `HTTPException(status_code=404, detail='Item not found')` for expected errors, and register exception handlers for custom exception classes to return a consistent error format. Validation errors are returned as 422 automatically. Log unexpected exceptions and return a generic 500 without leaking internals.")
q("fastapi", "How do I test a FastAPI app?", "Use `TestClient` (or `httpx.AsyncClient` for async) and override dependencies:\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\n\nclient = TestClient(app)\n\ndef test_create_task():\n    r = client.post('/tasks', json={'title': 'write docs'})\n    assert r.status_code == 201\n    assert r.json()['title'] == 'write docs'\n```")
q("node", "What is Node.js and how does it differ from the browser?", "Node.js runs JavaScript outside the browser using the V8 engine. It has no DOM or `window`, but provides modules for the file system, networking, processes and more. It uses a single-threaded event loop with non-blocking I/O, which makes it good for I/O-heavy servers; CPU-heavy work should go to worker threads or separate processes.")
q("node", "npm install vs npm ci?", "`npm install` resolves dependencies according to `package.json` and may update `package-lock.json`. `npm ci` installs exactly what the lockfile says, deletes `node_modules` first and fails if they disagree, so it is faster and reproducible for CI and deployment.")
q("node", "What does semantic versioning mean in package.json?", "Versions look like MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. Patch fixes bugs, minor adds backward-compatible features, major may break things. `^1.4.2` allows updates up to (but not including) 2.0.0; `~1.4.2` allows patch updates only. Commit the lockfile so installs are consistent.")
q("node", "How do I read environment variables safely in Node?", "Use `process.env.NAME` and validate at startup so the app fails fast with a clear message if something is missing. Keep secrets out of source control (use a `.env` file ignored by git locally and your platform's secret store in production) and never log them.")
q("node", "What's a stream in Node?", "A stream processes data in chunks instead of loading it all in memory, e.g. reading a large file with `fs.createReadStream` and piping it to a response. It keeps memory use low and lets you start sending data before the whole input is read. `stream.pipeline` handles errors and cleanup.")

# ---------------- Python language ----------------
q("python", "What's the difference between a list and a tuple?", "Both are ordered sequences. Lists are mutable (you can append, remove, change items); tuples are immutable, which makes them hashable if their items are, so they can be dictionary keys or set members. By convention, tuples hold fixed groups of different things (like a coordinate) and lists hold a variable number of similar things.")
q("python", "What are list comprehensions?", "A compact way to build a list from an iterable with an optional filter: `[x * x for x in range(10) if x % 2 == 0]`. There are also dictionary, set and generator versions. If the expression becomes complicated or has side effects, a normal loop is clearer.")
q("python", "What is a generator and why use one?", "A generator produces values lazily, one at a time, using `yield`, so it doesn't hold everything in memory.\n\n```python\ndef read_large(path):\n    with open(path) as f:\n        for line in f:\n            yield line.strip()\n```\n\nIt's ideal for large or infinite sequences and pipelines. A generator can only be consumed once.")
q("python", "What does `if __name__ == '__main__':` do?", "When you run a file directly, Python sets `__name__` to `'__main__'`; when the file is imported, it's the module's name. The guard lets a file work both as a script and as an importable module without running the script part on import.")
q("python", "What are *args and **kwargs?", "`*args` collects extra positional arguments into a tuple and `**kwargs` collects extra keyword arguments into a dict. They let functions accept flexible input or forward arguments to another function: `def wrapper(*args, **kwargs): return func(*args, **kwargs)`. In calls, `*` and `**` do the reverse and unpack.")
q("python", "Explain Python's GIL.", "The Global Interpreter Lock lets only one thread execute Python bytecode at a time in CPython, so threads don't speed up CPU-bound Python code. They still help with I/O-bound work, since the lock is released while waiting. For CPU-bound parallelism use `multiprocessing`, native extensions like NumPy, or (in newer versions) the free-threaded build.")
q("python", "is vs == in Python?", "`==` compares values for equality; `is` checks whether two names refer to the same object in memory. Use `is` for `None` (`if x is None`) and `==` for everything else. Small integers and interned strings may appear to work with `is`, but that's an implementation detail you shouldn't rely on.")
q("python", "How does Python handle default mutable arguments?", "The default value is created once, when the function is defined, so a default list or dict is shared between calls. Use `None` as the default and create the container inside: `def f(items=None): items = [] if items is None else items`.")
q("python", "What is a virtual environment and why use one?", "A virtual environment is an isolated Python installation with its own packages, so each project's dependencies don't conflict. Create one with `python -m venv .venv`, activate it, and `pip install -r requirements.txt`. Pin versions in a requirements file or lockfile for reproducibility.")
q("python", "What are decorators?", "A decorator is a function that takes a function and returns a modified one, applied with `@name` above a definition. Examples: `@functools.lru_cache` for caching, `@app.get('/x')` to register a route, and custom ones for logging or timing. `functools.wraps` preserves the original function's metadata.")
q("python", "What is the difference between a shallow and a deep copy?", "A shallow copy (`list(x)`, `x.copy()`, `copy.copy`) creates a new container but shares the nested objects, so mutating a nested list affects both. A deep copy (`copy.deepcopy`) recursively copies everything. Use deep copies when you need full independence of nested structures.")
q("python", "How should I handle exceptions in Python?", "Catch the specific exceptions you can handle, keep the `try` block small, and never use a bare `except:` that swallows everything including KeyboardInterrupt. Use `else` for code that should run only if no exception occurred, `finally` for cleanup, and `raise ... from err` to preserve the cause when converting exceptions. Let unexpected errors propagate so they're visible.")
q("python", "What are type hints and do they enforce anything?", "Annotations like `def area(r: float) -> float` document expected types and let tools such as mypy or pyright check them statically. Python itself doesn't enforce them at runtime. Libraries like Pydantic and FastAPI do use them to validate data.")
q("python", "What are f-strings and what can they do?", "f-strings embed expressions in string literals: `f\"{name} is {age + 1} next year\"`. They support format specs (`f\"{price:.2f}\"`, `f\"{n:,}\"`, `f\"{x:>8}\"`), and `f\"{value=}\"` prints the expression and its value for quick debugging.")
q("python", "What's the difference between a module, a package and a library?", "A module is a single `.py` file. A package is a directory of modules (with an `__init__.py` or as a namespace package). A library is a loose term for a collection of packages you install and import, usually from PyPI.")
q("python", "How do I read and write files properly in Python?", "Use `with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:` so the file closes automatically, specify the encoding explicitly, and use `pathlib.Path` for paths: `Path('data.txt').read_text(encoding='utf-8')`. For large files iterate line by line instead of reading everything.")
q("python", "What is the difference between `@staticmethod`, `@classmethod` and an instance method?", "An instance method receives `self` and works on one object. A class method receives the class as `cls`, which suits alternative constructors (`Date.from_string(...)`). A static method receives neither and is just a function namespaced inside the class.")
q("python", "What are context managers?", "Objects that set something up and reliably tear it down, used with `with`. Files, locks and database transactions are typical. You can write your own with a class that defines `__enter__` and `__exit__`, or with `@contextlib.contextmanager` and a `yield`.")
q("python", "When should I use asyncio?", "Use `asyncio` for many concurrent I/O-bound operations (network calls, web servers) where waiting dominates. It uses a single thread and cooperative scheduling via `await`. It doesn't speed up CPU-bound work, and one blocking call inside a coroutine stalls everything, so use async libraries throughout.")
q("python", "How do I profile slow Python code?", "Don't guess. Run `python -m cProfile -s cumtime script.py` to see where time goes, or use `timeit` for small snippets. Look at algorithmic problems first (nested loops, repeated work, wrong data structure), then consider caching, vectorising with NumPy, or moving hot loops to compiled code.")

for sub, question, answer, diff in QA:
    out.append(mk(CAT, "concept_" + sub, [("user", question), ("assistant", answer)], "c1_" + question[:25], diff, "authored", pick(R, SYS_GENERAL)))
    # a 'beginner' framing for a share of items: ask for the short version
    if len(answer) > 250 and R.random() < 0.5:
        first = answer.split("\n")[0]
        sentences = first.split(". ")
        short = ". ".join(sentences[:2]).rstrip(".") + "."
        out.append(mk(CAT, "concept_" + sub, [("user", f"I'm a beginner. {question[0].lower() + question[1:]} Keep it short."), ("assistant", short + " Ask if you want an example or more detail.")], "c1b_" + question[:25], "easy", "composed", pick(R, SYS_GENERAL)))

save("prog_concepts1", out)
